-- Q1052: IT General Controls [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Privilege creep (access accumulation) occurs when users retain permissions from previous roles after department transfers, violating the principle of least privilege under NIST SP 800-53 Section AC-2. Without periodic access recertification, users accumulate excessive privileges that create segregation-of-duties violations.
Wrong (B): Social engineering involves manipulating people into divulging information or granting access, not the gradual accumulation of system permissions through role transfers.
Wrong (C): Brute force accumulation is not a recognized security term; brute force refers to password-cracking attacks that systematically try combinations.
Wrong (D): Access token expiration relates to the time-limited validity of authentication tokens, not the retention of role-based permissions across department transfers.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1052;

-- Q1053: IT General Controls [hard/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): When ITGCs over logical access are ineffective, the auditor cannot rely on automated application controls within those systems because compromised access could allow unauthorized modifications to programs or data. Per PCAOB AS 2201.18, the auditor must expand substantive testing to determine whether material misstatements exist.
Wrong (A): The auditor cannot fully rely on automated controls when the underlying ITGCs are ineffective, since those controls depend on the integrity of the IT environment.
Wrong (C): Both automated and manual controls can be affected by ITGC weaknesses; automated controls are particularly vulnerable because they depend directly on system integrity.
Wrong (D): Weak ITGCs do not automatically require an adverse opinion; the auditor must first perform additional procedures to assess whether material misstatements actually exist.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1053;

-- Q1054: IT General Controls [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Developer write access to production databases without compensating controls violates segregation of duties per NIST SP 800-53 Section AC-5. Developers could modify data or programs without independent review, undermining data integrity and the reliability of processed financial information.
Wrong (A): Code reviews do not compensate for production database write access because the risk is unauthorized data modification, not code quality.
Wrong (C): The segregation-of-duties concern applies to all financial data processed by the system, not only databases storing personally identifiable information.
Wrong (D): Standard practice requires developers to have read-only or no access to production; unrestricted write access represents a pervasive control weakness regardless of debugging needs.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1054;

-- Q1055: IT General Controls [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under NIST SP 800-34 Section 5 (Contingency Planning Guide), restoring from incremental backups requires the most recent full backup plus all subsequent incrementals applied in chronological order. For a Thursday failure with Sunday full backups, the team needs Sunday''s full plus Monday through Thursday''s incrementals.
Wrong (A): Thursday''s incremental alone captures only changes since Wednesday and cannot restore the complete system state without the full backup and prior incrementals.
Wrong (C): Sunday''s full backup alone would restore the system only to its Sunday state, losing all data changes from Monday through Thursday.
Wrong (D): Incremental backups from the past month are unnecessary; only backups since the most recent full backup (Sunday) are needed for restoration.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1055;

