-- Q1206: SOC Testing Controls [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Reperformance is the most persuasive testing procedure because the auditor independently executes the control to verify it produces the expected results. Under AICPA attestation standards, the evidence hierarchy ranks reperformance above inspection, observation, and inquiry.
Wrong (A): Inquiry of management provides only testimonial evidence, which is the least persuasive form of evidence for testing operating effectiveness.
Wrong (B): Observation provides point-in-time evidence that the control was performed but does not verify the accuracy of the control''s output.
Wrong (C): Inspection of documentation confirms that records exist but does not independently verify that the control operated correctly when processing transactions.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1206;

-- Q1207: SOC Testing Controls [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): A SOC 2 Type I report evaluates the suitability of design of controls at a specific point in time, per AICPA AT-C Section 205. It assesses whether controls are appropriately designed to meet trust services criteria but does not test operating effectiveness over a period.
Wrong (B): Evaluating both design and operating effectiveness over a period of time describes a SOC 2 Type II report, not a Type I.
Wrong (C): A Type I report evaluates design effectiveness, not operating effectiveness; operating effectiveness is only tested in a Type II engagement.
Wrong (D): Assessing financial statement impact of control deficiencies is a SOC 1 report concern, not a SOC 2 Type I report focus.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1207;

-- Q1208: SOC Testing Controls [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): For daily manual controls over a 12-month period, a sample of 25 to 40 items spread across the full period provides reasonable assurance of operating effectiveness per AICPA SOC 2 sampling guidance (AT-C Section 205). Coverage must span the entire examination period for a period-wide conclusion.
Wrong (A): Selecting only 5 items from the last month provides insufficient coverage and cannot support a conclusion about year-round operating effectiveness across 12 months.
Wrong (C): Testing all 260 business days is impractical and unnecessary; statistical sampling provides reasonable assurance without requiring 100% testing of large populations.
Wrong (D): A single item cannot support a conclusion about consistent operation over 260 occurrences and provides no coverage of the remaining examination period.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1208;

-- Q1209: SOC Testing Controls [medium/L4]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per AICPA AT-C Section 205 (Examination Engagements), the auditor must evaluate each deviation''s nature, cause, and whether compensating controls mitigate the associated risk before reaching a conclusion. A deviation does not automatically result in a modified opinion.
Wrong (A): Automatically issuing a qualified opinion based solely on the deviation rate is incorrect because the auditor must assess whether the issue is systemic or isolated and evaluate compensating controls.
Wrong (C): Ignoring deviations is inappropriate regardless of the pass rate; each exception must be evaluated for its nature, cause, and impact on the control objective.
Wrong (D): Expanding to 40 additional items is not the immediate next step; the auditor must first evaluate the deviations found before deciding whether expanded testing is warranted.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1209;

