-- Q1212: SOC Testing Controls [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under the AICPA Guide: SOC 2 Reporting (Sampling Guidance for Small Populations), when a control population is very small, the practitioner should test all occurrences because sampling fewer items would not provide sufficient evidence of consistent operation. Testing the complete population eliminates sampling risk.
Wrong (A): Selecting only one item from a small population provides inadequate coverage and cannot support a conclusion about consistent operation across the full examination period.
Wrong (B): Two items from different sub-periods still leaves the majority of occurrences untested and does not provide sufficient evidence for a small population.
Wrong (D): Selecting 15 items exceeds the actual population of annual controls and is not a meaningful sampling approach when the total occurrences are very small.$EXPL$
