-- Q1216: SOC Reporting [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): A qualified opinion uses ''except for'' language to identify specific controls with exceptions while acknowledging that the overall control environment is effective. Under AT-C 205, this indicates limited exceptions that do not pervade the entire system.
Wrong (A): An unqualified opinion means no exceptions were found and the controls operated effectively throughout the period, which does not match this scenario.
Wrong (C): An adverse opinion indicates pervasive exceptions rendering the overall control environment ineffective, which is more severe than isolated access review failures.
Wrong (D): A disclaimer of opinion is issued when the practitioner cannot obtain sufficient evidence to form any opinion, not when specific exceptions are identified.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1216;

-- Q1217: SOC Reporting [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under the carve-out method per SSAE 18 (AT-C Section 320), the subservice organization''s controls are excluded from the SOC report scope. The report identifies the subservice organization and describes its functions but does not test its controls, requiring user auditors to obtain separate assurance.
Wrong (A): Including the subservice organization''s controls in the examination scope describes the inclusive method, which is the opposite of the carve-out approach.
Wrong (C): A carve-out report is not inherently less reliable; it simply limits scope, and user auditors obtain the subservice organization''s own SOC report to fill the gap.
Wrong (D): The carve-out method can be used regardless of how critical the subservice organization''s services are; criticality does not prevent issuance.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1217;

-- Q1218: SOC Reporting [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): CUECs are controls that the service organization assumes user entities will implement for the overall control environment to be effective, per AICPA SOC reporting guidance. Examples include timely disabling of terminated employee accounts and transaction authorization at the user entity level.
Wrong (A): A control implemented by the service organization on behalf of user entities is the service organization''s own control, not a complementary user entity control.
Wrong (C): A CUEC is not a backup control; it is a required user entity responsibility that must be in place for the service organization''s controls to achieve their objectives.
